@database "markdown_formatter"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: markdown_formatter.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Markdown Formatter"
@{h1}Markdown Formatter

Makes an agent's answers readable where they are shown. Models write
Markdown; without this plugin the web chat shows it as plain text, with the
asterisks and pipes. With it, the answer arrives in the chat as HTML --
headings, lists, tables, code blocks coloured by the browser -- and in the
terminal it is drawn with colours. What is stored in the session stays the
Markdown the model wrote; only the copy that is shown is converted.

A second, optional hook asks the model to write Markdown in the first
place, by adding a short instruction to its system prompt.

The plugin has no tools and no panel. It is switched on in the shipped
configuration, but both hooks are off for every agent until the agent's
YAML turns one on.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  What you see where  " link where}
  @{"  Limits and safety  " link limits}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The format hook  " link format}
  @{"  The prompt hook  " link prompt}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    markdown_formatter:
      type: markdown_formatter
      enabled: true
@{body}

No allowlist line is needed -- the plugin has no tools. It needs the
Python packages @{tt}markdown@{utt} (the conversion to HTML) and
@{tt}markdownify@{utt} (HTML back to Markdown for the terminal, see
@{" The format hook " link format}); both come with the installation.

@{h2}Switching the hooks on for an agent

The @{tt}default_config@{utt} at the top of @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}
switches both hooks off for all agents:

@{code yaml}
      hooks:
        enabled: true
        overrides:
          markdown_formatter.format_markdown_output:
             enabled: false
          markdown_formatter.inject_markdown_system_prompt:
             enabled: false
@{body}

An agent turns a hook on in its own YAML; the two lists are merged, so the
other hook stays off:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    enabled: true
    overrides:
      markdown_formatter.format_markdown_output:
        enabled: true
@{body}

Most agents that talk to people -- the chat agents, the sysadmin, the
coder, the research agent -- do this for the format hook. No shipped agent
turns on the prompt hook.

@{h2}Settings

The settings go in a @{tt}config:@{utt} block of the server entry. They are
read once, when the plugin starts, and hold for every agent; an agent's
override can only switch a hook on or off.

@{code yaml}
    markdown_formatter:
      type: markdown_formatter
      enabled: true
      config:
        enable_tables: false
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
convert_to_html | true | false: the web chat gets the Markdown as plain text
enable_tables | true | Markdown tables become HTML tables
enable_code_highlighting | true | fenced code blocks are recognised and marked with their language for the browser's colouring; false: a fence becomes inline code
sanitize_html | true | clean the HTML before it reaches the browser -- see @{" Limits and safety " link limits}
allowed_html_tags | headings, p, br, hr, strong, em, code, pre, ul, ol, li, table, thead, tbody, tr, th, td, a, blockquote | the tags the cleaning keeps
inject_system_prompt | true | false: the prompt hook does nothing even where it is on
system_prompt_template | see @{" The prompt hook " link prompt} | the instruction the prompt hook adds
@{body}
@endnode

@node where "What you see where"
@{h1}What you see where

@{h2}Web chat

Each step's text streams in as HTML, and so does the final answer: headings,
lists and tables as such, code blocks with their language so the browser
colours them. A single line break in the answer stays a line break. A list
the model wrote into one line ("Options - a - b - c") is split into a list.
An answer that is wholly wrapped in a @{tt}```markdown@{utt} code block is
unwrapped first.

When a session is opened again, its answers are converted anew, with the
hooks of the agent that held the session; if that agent is not loaded, the
history shows as plain text. Answers of a run that asked for structured
output (JSON) are never converted.

Without the hook -- off for the agent, @{tt}convert_to_html: false@{utt},
or the @{tt}markdown@{utt} package missing -- the chat shows the text as it
is, escaped, with its line breaks.

@{h2}Terminal

@{tt}agent-cli@{utt} and @{tt}agent-run@{utt} ask for the format their
@{tt}--color@{utt} option names. With colour (@{tt}auto@{utt} on a terminal
that can show it, @{tt}always@{utt}, @{tt}ansi@{utt}), the hook hands the
Markdown back and the terminal draws it with colours. With
@{tt}never@{utt} or @{tt}text@{utt} the Markdown is printed as it is. A
wrapping @{tt}```markdown@{utt} block is removed in both cases.
@endnode

@node limits "Limits and safety"
@{h1}Limits and safety

@{h2}What the cleaning removes

A model's answer carries whatever a tool fetched, including HTML someone
else wrote. With @{tt}sanitize_html: true@{utt} only the allowed tags stay,
each with only a few attributes (a link's address and title, a code block's
language, a list's start number); a link to anything but an http, https
or mailto address (javascript:, data:) loses its address. Script and style blocks are removed with their content; other
tags lose the tag and keep their text. There are no images by default: a
remote image is a request the reader's browser makes to an address the
text chose.

@{b}sanitize_html: false@{ub} hands the model's raw HTML to the browser as
it is, scripts included. Leave it on. Adding a tag to
@{tt}allowed_html_tags@{utt} lets it through with the attributes listed
above only -- @{tt}img@{utt} with its @{tt}src@{utt}.

@{h2}Cost

The conversion runs in a worker thread, so the server goes on answering
while it runs. Measured on this machine:

@{table}
Answer | Time
a few kilobytes | milliseconds
1 MB of ordinary Markdown (headings, tables, code) | 5 s
200 KB cut off inside a code block (no closing fence) | 0.1 s
200 KB of 100,000 short lines in one paragraph | stopped after 10 s
@{body}

A paragraph made of very many lines costs the Markdown library time that
grows with the square of the line count (20,000 lines: 1 s; 100,000: about
48 s). The hook's 10-second timeout ends the wait for that answer: it is
then shown as plain text. The thread itself runs on to the end, and other
conversions wait for it meanwhile. The same text is converted again each
time the session is opened.
@endnode

@node format "The format hook"
@{h1}The format hook

@{tt}format_markdown_output@{utt}, type @{tt}format_output@{utt}. It is
called by whoever shows an answer, with the format wanted, and changes only
that copy -- never the message in the session:

@{bullet}the agent loop, for every step's text, as HTML;
@{bullet}the API, for each answer of a session opened again, as HTML;
@{bullet}the terminal clients, for the final answer, as ansi or text.

First, an answer wholly wrapped in @{tt}```markdown@{utt} or
@{tt}```md@{utt} is unwrapped. Then, by format:

@{table}
Format | What it returns | content_format
html | the HTML; unchanged if @{tt}convert_to_html@{utt} is false or the conversion is not possible | html (text when unchanged)
ansi | the Markdown, for the terminal to draw; text that starts with < and contains </p>, </h1> or </h2> is taken for HTML and turned back into Markdown | ansi
text | the Markdown; text that starts with < and contains <p>, <h1> or <pre> is taken for HTML and reduced to plain text | text
anything else | the text unchanged | the format asked for
@{body}

The HTML path uses the renderer shared with the debate forum
(@{tt}agent_system/utils/markdown_render.py@{utt}): tables, fenced code with
@{tt}class="language-..."@{utt}, single line breaks kept, the one-line list
rescue, then the cleaning described in
@{" Limits and safety " link limits}. Links are what Markdown writes as
links -- @{tt}[text](address)@{utt} and @{tt}<https://...>@{utt}; a bare
address stays text.

The HTML check on the ansi and text paths exists for text that is HTML
already. An answer that is itself HTML the model wrote unfenced and that
starts with a tag is converted along with it.

An error inside the hook is logged and the answer is shown unchanged.
@endnode

@node prompt "The prompt hook"
@{h1}The prompt hook

@{tt}inject_markdown_system_prompt@{utt}, type @{tt}pre_llm_call@{utt}.
Before every LLM call of the agent -- every step, not once per session --
it adds this text, the default of @{tt}system_prompt_template@{utt}:

@{code}
Format your responses using Markdown for better readability:
- DO NOT wrap the complete response in markdown code blocks.
- Use tables and lists where appropriate.
- Put code snippets in triple backtick blocks with language specified.
@{body}

@{bullet}If the call has system messages, the text is appended to the
@{b}first@{ub} one -- the agent's own prompt, which the agent loop renders
anew before each step -- after a blank line, unless that message contains
it already. The other system messages and the conversation are not
touched.
@{bullet}If there is no system message, the text becomes one, at the start.
@{bullet}With @{tt}inject_system_prompt: false@{utt} it does nothing.

System messages are built fresh for each run and are not saved with the
session, so the text is never stored and never doubles. The hook runs
before @{tt}message_validation@{utt}. It runs for every agent that turns it
on, sub-agents included; the chat is an agent like any other.

It is worth turning on for a model that answers in plain prose or wraps
everything in a code block. Most models write Markdown unasked.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{b}The format hook@{ub} changes nothing the model sees: it works on the
copy that is shown, after the model has answered. The session keeps the
Markdown, so the next call sends the model its own words, not HTML. It
costs no tokens.

@{b}The prompt hook@{ub} adds the instruction in
@{" The prompt hook " link prompt} to the first system message, about
60 tokens with the default text, on every call. The text is the same at
every step and in every run, so the system prompt stays one fixed prefix
and the provider's prompt cache keeps working; the hook rewrites no
earlier message. A template changed in the settings changes that prefix
once, at the next start.

Neither hook reads or changes tool calls, their arguments or tool results.
@endnode
